1
00:00:01,879 --> 00:00:05,319
Sentinels were placed at all the approaches to the farm.

2
00:00:05,319 --> 00:00:10,159
In addition, four pigeons were sent to Foxwood with a conciliatory message,

3
00:00:10,159 --> 00:00:15,840
which it was hoped might reestablish good relations with Pilkington.

4
00:00:15,879 --> 00:00:19,440
The very next morning, the attack came.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,920
The animals were at breakfast when the lookouts came racing in with the news

6
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:28,120
that Frederick and his followers had already come through the five-barred gate.

7
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:33,079
Boldly enough, the animals sallied forth to meet them, but this time they did not

8
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:37,399
have the easy victory that they had had in the Battle of the Cowshed.

9
00:00:37,399 --> 00:00:42,319
There were 15 men with half a dozen guns between them, and they opened fire as

10
00:00:42,319 --> 00:00:45,360
soon as they got within 50 yards.

11
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:50,000
The animals could not face the terrible explosions and the stinging pellets, and

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
in spite of the efforts of Napoleon and Boxer to rally them, they were soon driven back.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,600
A number of them were already wounded.

14
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:05,000
They took refuge in the farm buildings and peeped cautiously out from chinks and knotholes.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,440
The whole of the big pasture, including the windmill, was in the hands of the enemy.

16
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:14,280
For the moment, even Napoleon seemed at a loss.

17
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:19,760
He paced up and down without a word, his tail rigid and twitching.

18
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:23,719
Wistful glances were sent in the direction of Foxwood.

19
00:01:23,719 --> 00:01:28,559
If Pilkington and his men would help them, the day might yet be won.

20
00:01:28,559 --> 00:01:34,319
But at this moment, the four pigeons who had been sent out on the day before returned.

21
00:01:34,319 --> 00:01:38,120
One of them bearing a scrap of paper from Pilkington.

22
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:42,440
On it was penciled the words, >> "Serves you right."

23
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:46,159
>> Meanwhile, Frederick and his men had halted about the windmill.

24
00:01:46,159 --> 00:01:50,760
The animals watched them and a murmur of dismay went round.

25
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:54,319
Two of the men had produced a crowbar and a sledgehammer.

26
00:01:54,319 --> 00:01:56,959
They were going to knock the windmill down.

27
00:01:56,959 --> 00:02:01,280
>> "Impossible! We have built the walls far too thick for that. They could not knock

28
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,600
it down in a week. Courage, comrades."

29
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:08,520
>> But Benjamin was watching the movements of the men intently.

30
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,680
The two with the hammer and the crowbar were drilling a hole near the base of the windmill.

31
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:19,920
Slowly and with an air almost of amusement, Benjamin nodded his long muzzle.

32
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,520
>> "I thought so.

33
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,680
Do you not see what they are doing?

34
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:28,200
In another moment, they are going to pack blasting powder into that hole."

35
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,040
>> Terrified, the animals waited.

36
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:35,719
It was impossible now to venture out of the shelter of the buildings.

37
00:02:35,719 --> 00:02:40,639
After a few minutes, the men were seen to be running in all directions.

38
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:44,200
Then, there was a deafening roar.

39
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:49,840
The pigeons swirled into the air and all the animals, except Napoleon, flung

40
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:54,479
themselves flat on their bellies and and hid their faces.

41
00:02:54,479 --> 00:03:01,239
When they got up again, a huge cloud of black smoke was hanging where the windmill had been.

42
00:03:01,239 --> 00:03:05,120
Slowly, the breeze drifted it away.

43
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:08,920
The windmill had ceased to exist.

44
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:12,440
At this sight, the animals' courage returned to them.

45
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:16,319
The fear and despair they had felt a moment earlier were drowned in their

46
00:03:16,319 --> 00:03:20,479
rage against this vile, contemptible act.

47
00:03:20,479 --> 00:03:25,399
A mighty cry for vengeance went up, and without waiting for further orders, they

48
00:03:25,399 --> 00:03:29,680
charged forth in a body and made straight for the enemy.

49
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:34,879
This time, they did not heed the cruel pellets that swept over them like hail.

50
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:37,959
It was a savage, bitter battle.

51
00:03:37,959 --> 00:03:43,399
The men fired again and again, and when the animals got to close quarters,

52
00:03:43,399 --> 00:03:47,319
lashed out with their sticks and their heavy boots.

53
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:53,200
A cow, three sheep, and two geese were killed, and nearly everyone was wounded.

54
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,399
Even Napoleon, who was directing operations from the rear, had the tip of

55
00:03:57,399 --> 00:04:00,200
his tail chipped by a pellet.

56
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:03,520
But the men did not go unscathed, either.

57
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:08,280
Three of them had their heads broken by blows from Boxer's hooves. Another was

58
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:10,959
gored in the belly by a cow's horn.

59
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:15,479
Another had his trousers nearly torn off by Jessie and Bluebell.

60
00:04:15,479 --> 00:04:20,040
And when the nine dogs of Napoleon's own bodyguard, whom he had instructed to

61
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:24,440
make a detour under cover of the hedge, suddenly appeared on the men's flank,

62
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:28,600
baying ferociously, panic overtook them.

63
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,959
They saw that they were in danger of being surrounded.

64
00:04:31,959 --> 00:04:36,239
Frederick shouted to his men to get out while the going was good, and the next

65
00:04:36,239 --> 00:04:40,760
moment, the cowardly enemy was running for dear life.

66
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:44,840
The animals chased them right down to the bottom of the field and got in some

67
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:49,600
last kicks at them as they forced their way through the thorn hedge.

68
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:53,440
They had won, but they were weary and bleeding.

69
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:56,799
Slowly they began to limp back towards the farm.

70
00:04:56,799 --> 00:05:01,840
The sight of their dead comrades stretched upon the grass moved some of them to tears.

71
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:05,639
And for a little while they halted in sorrowful silence at the place where the

72
00:05:05,639 --> 00:05:08,000
windmill had once stood.

73
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,040
Yes, it was gone.

74
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:13,079
Almost the last trace of their labor was gone.

75
00:05:13,079 --> 00:05:16,079
Even the foundations were partially destroyed.

76
00:05:16,079 --> 00:05:17,000
And in rebuilding it, they could not
